window.AILETMOCK={"n":1,"title":"Mock Test 1","theme":"Basic structure and elections","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"Basic structure, privacy, electoral bonds, Article 370, SC/ST sub-classification and Governors' assent in constitutional law. Seven more branches of law and contemporary legal GK complete the paper.","minutes":120,"neg":0.25,"sec":["Constitutional Law","Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","International Law and Human Rights","Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK"],"q":[{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Basic Structure","q":"The basic structure doctrine, limiting Parliament's power to amend the Constitution under Article 368, was laid down in:","o":["Golak Nath v. State of Punjab (1967)","Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973)","A.K. Gopalan v. State of Madras (1950)","Minerva Mills v. Union of India (1980)"],"a":1,"e":"A 13-judge bench in Kesavananda Bharati (7:6) held that Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution but cannot alter its basic structure. Golak Nath had held fundamental rights unamendable and was overruled; Minerva Mills later applied the doctrine to strike down parts of the 42nd Amendment."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Fundamental Rights","q":"Article 21A, guaranteeing free and compulsory education to children aged 6 to 14, was inserted by the:","o":["73rd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992","42nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1976","86th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2002","44th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1978"],"a":2,"e":"The 86th Amendment (2002) inserted Article 21A, amended Article 45 and added Article 51A(k). The 42nd added Fundamental Duties, the 44th removed property as a fundamental right, and the 73rd dealt with Panchayati Raj."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Privacy","q":"The right to privacy was declared a fundamental right in K.S. Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017) by a bench of how many judges?","o":["Seven","Five","Nine","Eleven"],"a":2,"e":"Puttaswamy was decided by a nine-judge bench, which unanimously held privacy to be intrinsic to life and personal liberty under Article 21 and to the freedoms guaranteed in Part III."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Electoral Bonds","q":"In Association for Democratic Reforms v. Union of India (2024), the Supreme Court struck down the Electoral Bond Scheme primarily because it:","o":["contravened the freedom of inter-state trade under Article 303","breached the Election Commission's powers under Article 324","exceeded Parliament's legislative competence under the Seventh Schedule","violated the voters' right to information under Article 19(1)(a)"],"a":3,"e":"A five-judge bench (Feb 2024) held that anonymous political funding through bonds infringed the citizens' right to information, a facet of Article 19(1)(a), and that the related amendments to the Representation of the People Act, Companies Act and Income Tax Act were unconstitutional."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Article 370","q":"In In re: Article 370 of the Constitution (December 2023), the Constitution Bench held that:","o":["the abrogation was invalid without a recommendation of the State's Constituent Assembly","Jammu and Kashmir retained internal sovereignty that the Constitution did not subsume","Article 370 was a temporary provision and the President could validly end its operation","Article 370 became permanent once the State's Constituent Assembly was dissolved"],"a":2,"e":"The unanimous verdict upheld the 2019 Presidential Orders, holding that Article 370 was temporary and that J&K did not retain sovereignty after accession. The other options were arguments of the petitioners that the Court rejected."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"SC/ST Sub-classification","q":"In State of Punjab v. Davinder Singh (2024), a seven-judge bench, in permitting sub-classification of Scheduled Castes, overruled:","o":["E.V. Chinnaiah v. State of Andhra Pradesh (2004)","M. Nagaraj v. Union of India (2006)","Ashoka Kumar Thakur v. Union of India (2008)","Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992)"],"a":0,"e":"Davinder Singh (Aug 2024) held that States may sub-classify Scheduled Castes on the basis of empirical data to give more benefit to the most backward, overruling E.V. Chinnaiah, which treated SCs as a homogeneous class."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Governor's Assent","q":"In the Presidential Reference under Article 143 on Governors' and the President's assent to State Bills (opinion of November 2025), the Supreme Court held that:","o":["the President's decision under Article 201 is open to full review on its merits","courts cannot fix rigid timelines or recognise 'deemed assent', though prolonged inaction remains reviewable in a limited way","a Governor must decide every Bill within three months, failing which assent is deemed granted","Article 361 immunity bars all judicial scrutiny of a Governor's delay in acting on Bills"],"a":1,"e":"The Court's advisory opinion departed from the April 2025 Tamil Nadu v. Governor approach of judicially fixed timelines and deemed assent, holding that such concepts are inconsistent with the constitutional scheme while preserving limited judicial review of inordinate, unexplained delay. (Verify the latest position before the exam.)"},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Article 356","q":"Which decision held that a Presidential Proclamation under Article 356 is subject to judicial review and that secularism is part of the basic structure?","o":["Indira Nehru Gandhi v. Raj Narain (1975)","S.R. Bommai v. Union of India (1994)","L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India (1997)","Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973)"],"a":1,"e":"A nine-judge bench in S.R. Bommai laid down that the satisfaction of the President is justiciable, that floor test is the proper way to test majority, and that secularism forms part of the basic structure."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Anti-Defection","q":"Under the Tenth Schedule, a question of disqualification of a legislator on the ground of defection is decided by:","o":["the Election Commission of India, acting independently","the President, after consulting the Election Commission","the High Court having jurisdiction over the State","the Speaker or Chairman of the House concerned"],"a":3,"e":"Paragraph 6 of the Tenth Schedule vests the decision in the presiding officer, whose decision is subject to limited judicial review (Kihoto Hollohan, 1992). The President-on-ECI-advice route applies to other disqualifications under Article 103."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Money Bills","q":"A Money Bill passed by the Lok Sabha and transmitted to the Rajya Sabha must be returned by the Rajya Sabha with its recommendations within:","o":["14 days","21 days","30 days","7 days"],"a":0,"e":"Under Article 109, the Rajya Sabha must return a Money Bill within 14 days; the Lok Sabha may accept or reject its recommendations. If not returned, the Bill is deemed passed."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Writs","q":"X has been holding a public office without any legal authority to do so. The writ that can challenge his right to hold that office is:","o":["Mandamus","Quo warranto","Prohibition","Certiorari"],"a":1,"e":"Quo warranto ('by what authority') tests the legality of a person's claim to a public office. Mandamus commands performance of a public duty, certiorari quashes a decision of an inferior body, and prohibition restrains it from exceeding jurisdiction."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Article 14","q":"The 'new concept' of equality, under which arbitrariness is antithetical to Article 14, was articulated in:","o":["State of West Bengal v. Anwar Ali Sarkar (1952)","Ram Krishna Dalmia v. Justice S.R. Tendolkar (1958)","E.P. Royappa v. State of Tamil Nadu (1974)","Budhan Choudhry v. State of Bihar (1955)"],"a":2,"e":"Royappa (and later Maneka Gandhi) held equality and arbitrariness to be sworn enemies. The other cases concern the traditional reasonable-classification test (intelligible differentia with a rational nexus to the object)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Free Speech","q":"In Shreya Singhal v. Union of India (2015), the Supreme Court struck down:","o":["Section 66A of the Information Technology Act, 2000","Section 79 of the Information Technology Act, 2000","Section 43A of the Information Technology Act, 2000","Section 69A of the Information Technology Act, 2000"],"a":0,"e":"Section 66A was struck down as vague and overbroad, violating Article 19(1)(a) and falling outside Article 19(2). Section 69A (blocking) was upheld, and Section 79 was read down."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"EWS Reservation","q":"The 103rd Constitutional Amendment (2019), which inserted Articles 15(6) and 16(6), provides reservation for:","o":["economically weaker sections not covered by existing SC, ST and OBC reservation","sub-categories within the Other Backward Classes list","women in the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies","the Anglo-Indian community in Parliament and State legislatures"],"a":0,"e":"The amendment was upheld by a 3:2 majority in Janhit Abhiyan v. Union of India (2022). Women's reservation came through the 106th Amendment; Anglo-Indian nomination was ended by the 104th Amendment."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Women's Reservation","q":"The Constitution (106th Amendment) Act, 2023 (Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam) reserves one-third of seats for women in:","o":["Panchayats and Municipalities, replacing earlier provisions","the Lok Sabha, the Rajya Sabha and State Legislative Councils","the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies, including within SC and ST seats","the Rajya Sabha and State Legislative Councils only"],"a":2,"e":"The reservation covers the Lok Sabha and Legislative Assemblies (including Delhi) and applies after the first census and delimitation following commencement. Rajya Sabha and Legislative Councils are not covered; local-body reservation already exists under Articles 243D and 243T."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Judicial Appointments","q":"In Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India (2015), the Supreme Court struck down:","o":["the 97th Amendment, which dealt with co-operative societies","the 101st Amendment, which introduced the GST regime","the 99th Amendment and the NJAC Act, 2014","the 103rd Amendment, which created EWS reservation"],"a":2,"e":"A five-judge bench (4:1) held that the NJAC compromised the independence of the judiciary, a basic feature, and revived the Collegium system."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Same-Sex Marriage","q":"In Supriyo @ Supriya Chakraborty v. Union of India (2023), the Constitution Bench held that:","o":["the Special Marriage Act, 1954 must be read as gender-neutral to include same-sex couples","there is no fundamental right to marry, and recognising same-sex marriage is for the legislature","Section 4 of the Special Marriage Act, 1954 is unconstitutional for discriminating on sexual orientation","same-sex couples have an enforceable fundamental right to a civil union"],"a":1,"e":"The Court declined to read down or strike the Special Marriage Act and left the matter to Parliament, while recording the Union's assurance to form a committee on rights of queer couples."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Citizenship","q":"In 2024 a Constitution Bench upheld Section 6A of the Citizenship Act, 1955 (4:1). It concerns citizenship of migrants who entered Assam from Bangladesh:","o":["on or before 31 December 2014, if non-Muslim","after 25 March 1971 and before 2014","before 15 August 1947 only","between 1 January 1966 and 25 March 1971"],"a":3,"e":"Section 6A, inserted after the 1985 Assam Accord, gives citizenship to those who entered before 1 January 1966 and a conditional route to those who entered between 1966 and 25 March 1971. The 2014 cut-off belongs to the Citizenship (Amendment) Act, 2019."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"State Action","q":"In its November 2024 directions on demolitions ('bulldozer justice'), the Supreme Court required ordinarily at least how much prior notice to the owner or occupier?","o":["Fifteen days","Thirty days","Seven days","Forty-eight hours"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that demolition of property merely because of accusation of crime is arbitrary and violative of the rule of law, and mandated a written notice of 15 days, an opportunity to respond, a reasoned order and video-recording of the demolition (public roads and unauthorised encroachments excepted)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Federalism","q":"The doctrine of pith and substance is applied by courts to determine:","o":["whether a law abridges a fundamental right guaranteed by Part III","whether a law falls within the legislative competence of the enacting legislature","whether legislative power has been excessively delegated to the executive","whether an invalid part of a statute can be separated from the valid part"],"a":1,"e":"Where a law appears to overlap entries in the Union, State and Concurrent Lists, the court looks at its true nature and character; incidental encroachment does not invalidate it."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Fundamental Duties","q":"Fundamental Duties were added to the Constitution by the 42nd Amendment on the recommendation of the:","o":["Venkatachaliah Commission","Swaran Singh Committee","Balwant Rai Mehta Committee","Sarkaria Commission"],"a":1,"e":"The Swaran Singh Committee (1976) recommended the duties, now in Article 51A (originally ten, expanded to eleven by the 86th Amendment)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Article 21","q":"In Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978), the Supreme Court held that the 'procedure established by law' under Article 21 must be:","o":["the same as the due process standard of the United States, including substantive review","whatever procedure is prescribed by a competent legislature, without review of its fairness","just, fair and reasonable, not arbitrary or oppressive","consistent with the common law procedure followed in England"],"a":2,"e":"Maneka Gandhi read Articles 14, 19 and 21 together (the 'golden triangle'), importing fairness into the procedure and overruling the narrow view in A.K. Gopalan."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Elections","q":"The Constitution (129th Amendment) Bill, 2024, which proposes simultaneous elections, seeks to insert which new Article?","o":["Article 82A","Article 324A","Article 83A","Article 368A"],"a":0,"e":"The Bill proposes Article 82A on simultaneous elections to the Lok Sabha and State Assemblies and amends Articles 83, 172 and 327. It was introduced in December 2024 and referred to a Joint Parliamentary Committee."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Supreme Court Powers","q":"Which Article empowers the Supreme Court to pass any decree or order necessary for doing 'complete justice' in a matter before it?","o":["Article 142","Article 226","Article 141","Article 136"],"a":0,"e":"Article 142(1) is a plenary power to do complete justice, used for example in the Union Carbide settlement matter; it cannot, however, override express statutory prohibitions. Article 136 is special leave, Article 141 makes SC law binding, and Article 226 concerns High Court writs."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"The Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 replaced the Indian Penal Code and came into force on:","o":["1 January 2025","1 July 2024","25 December 2023","26 January 2024"],"a":1,"e":"The three criminal law Acts (BNS, BNSS, BSA) received Presidential assent on 25 December 2023 but were brought into force on 1 July 2024."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, the punishment for murder is prescribed in Section:","o":["103","105","302","100"],"a":0,"e":"Section 101 defines murder and Section 103 prescribes its punishment (death or life imprisonment, and fine). Section 100 defines culpable homicide, Section 105 punishes culpable homicide not amounting to murder, and Section 302 was the old IPC provision."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"The BNS provision that replaced the IPC offence of sedition (Section 124A) is Section:","o":["111","152","113","147"],"a":1,"e":"Section 152 punishes acts endangering the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India. Section 111 is organised crime, 113 is terrorist act and 147 is waging war against the Government of India."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under the BNS, murder committed by a group of five or more persons acting together on the ground of race, caste, community, sex, place of birth, language or personal belief is punishable with:","o":["imprisonment for life only, without the option of death","imprisonment of not less than seven years, with fine","death or imprisonment for life, and fine","rigorous imprisonment up to ten years, and fine"],"a":2,"e":"Section 103(2) BNS creates the offence of mob lynching as an aggravated form of murder."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under Section 187 of the BNSS, police custody of an accused may be authorised for a total of up to 15 days, which can be availed:","o":["in whole or in parts, during the first 40 or 60 days of detention","only during the first 24 hours of arrest","only continuously, from the date of arrest","for up to 30 days within the first 60 or 90 days"],"a":0,"e":"The BNSS lets the 15-day police custody be spread across the first 40 days (offences with up to 10 years) or first 60 days (more serious offences), a change from the CrPC reading that confined it to the first 15 days."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under the BNSS, when an electronic information about a cognizable offence is given, the informant must sign it within:","o":["seven days","three days","24 hours","fifteen days"],"a":1,"e":"Section 173(1) BNSS allows e-FIR, which is recorded on the informant's signing within three days. It also codifies Zero FIR, registered at any police station irrespective of jurisdiction."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"The BNSS requires a court to pronounce its judgment within how many days of the conclusion of arguments (extendable up to 60 days in writing)?","o":["15 days","90 days","45 days","30 days"],"a":2,"e":"Section 258 BNSS prescribes that judgment be delivered within 45 days from the completion of arguments, a time limit that did not exist in the CrPC."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BSA","q":"The Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023, which replaced the Indian Evidence Act, 1872, contains how many sections?","o":["531","167","358","170"],"a":3,"e":"The BSA has 170 sections (the Evidence Act had 167). The BNS has 358 sections and the BNSS has 531. The BSA expressly treats electronic and digital records as documents."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Which of the following offences can be punished with community service under the BNS, a new form of punishment?","o":["Mischief causing damage to a railway","Cheating that induces delivery of property","Criminal breach of trust by a public servant","Defamation"],"a":3,"e":"Community service is prescribed for petty offences such as defamation (Section 356(2)), theft of low-value property by a first-time offender who restores it, public intoxication causing annoyance, and certain attempts to compel public servants by threat of suicide."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS Application","q":"A fires at B intending to kill B, but the bullet misses B and kills C, a passer-by. Applying the doctrine of transferred malice, A is:","o":["guilty of culpable homicide not amounting to murder, as C's death was unintended","guilty of murder of C, as his intention transfers to the person actually killed","not liable for C's death, since he neither intended nor foresaw it","guilty only of attempt to murder B, since he had no intent regarding C"],"a":1,"e":"Under the transferred malice principle, the criminal intention aimed at one person is transferred to the person actually harmed. A is guilty of murder of C, and may additionally be liable for attempt on B."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"General Exceptions","q":"For the defence of unsoundness of mind (insanity) under the BNS, the relevant time at which the accused's mental state is assessed is:","o":["the time of the trial","the year preceding the offence","the time of his arrest","the time of commission of the act"],"a":3,"e":"The defence applies if, at the time of doing the act, the accused by reason of unsoundness of mind was incapable of knowing the nature of the act or that it was wrong or contrary to law. Fitness to stand trial is a separate procedural question."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Arrest","q":"Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar (2014) laid down safeguards against routine arrest for offences punishable with imprisonment:","o":["up to seven years","of more than seven years","up to three years only","for life only"],"a":0,"e":"The Court directed police to issue a notice of appearance rather than arrest automatically, and Magistrates not to authorise detention mechanically. The principle now maps onto Section 35 of the BNSS."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Self-Incrimination","q":"In Selvi v. State of Karnataka (2010), the Supreme Court held that involuntary narco-analysis, polygraph and brain-mapping tests violate:","o":["the guarantee of equality before the law under Article 14 alone","the protection against double jeopardy under Article 20(2)","the protection against self-incrimination under Articles 20(3) and 21","the freedom of speech and expression under Article 19(1)(a) alone"],"a":2,"e":"Compelled administration of these techniques was held to be testimonial compulsion and an intrusion into mental privacy; voluntary administration with safeguards is permissible."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Death Penalty","q":"The 'rarest of rare' doctrine for imposing the death penalty was laid down in:","o":["Bachan Singh v. State of Punjab (1980)","Machhi Singh v. State of Punjab (1983)","Jagmohan Singh v. State of U.P. (1973)","Mithu v. State of Punjab (1983)"],"a":0,"e":"Bachan Singh upheld the constitutionality of the death penalty but restricted it to the rarest of rare cases, with life imprisonment the rule. Mithu struck down the mandatory death penalty under old IPC Section 303."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Contract","q":"In Carlill v. Carbolic Smoke Ball Co., the advertisement promising a reward was held to be:","o":["a mere invitation to treat, since it was addressed to the general public","a unilateral offer to the world at large, accepted by performing its conditions","a bilateral contract needing prior communication of acceptance to the company","an invitation to negotiate, being a puff not meant to bind"],"a":1,"e":"The deposit of money with a bank showed intention to create legal relations, and acceptance by conduct (use of the smoke ball) sufficed, since the offeror dispensed with notification."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Contract","q":"Under the Indian Contract Act, 1872, an agreement with a minor is:","o":["void ab initio","voidable at the minor's option","valid, and ratifiable on attaining majority","valid when it is beneficial to the minor"],"a":0,"e":"Mohori Bibee v. Dharmodas Ghose (1903) held that a minor's agreement is absolutely void and cannot be ratified; the minor's estate is liable only for necessaries (Section 68)."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Contract","q":"The rule in Hadley v. Baxendale is relevant to which aspect of contract law?","o":["Consideration moving from a third party","Frustration through supervening impossibility","Coercion by unlawful threats","Remoteness of damage for breach"],"a":3,"e":"The rule, reflected in Section 73 of the Contract Act, limits damages to losses arising naturally from the breach or reasonably in the contemplation of both parties at the time of contracting."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Contract","q":"A agrees to sell specific goods to B. Before delivery, the goods are destroyed by fire without fault of either party. The agreement is:","o":["voidable at the option of the buyer B","void, because performance has become impossible","valid, and A must pay damages to B","enforceable by delivery of similar substitute goods"],"a":1,"e":"Section 56 (frustration) and Section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act render the agreement void where specific goods perish without the seller's fault before the risk passes."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Contract","q":"After the Specific Relief (Amendment) Act, 2018, specific performance of a contract is:","o":["a remedy available only against the Government","a remedy barred for all commercial contracts","a discretionary remedy available only where damages are inadequate","a general rule, subject to specified exceptions"],"a":3,"e":"The amendment substituted Section 10 so that specific performance is no longer discretionary, and restricted injunctions in infrastructure project contracts. The older 'discretionary' framing no longer applies."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Torts","q":"The 'neighbour principle' in the law of negligence originated in:","o":["Rylands v. Fletcher (1868)","Caparo Industries v. Dickman (1990)","Donoghue v. Stevenson (1932)","Bolton v. Stone (1951)"],"a":2,"e":"Lord Atkin formulated the principle that one must take reasonable care to avoid acts or omissions that can reasonably be foreseen to injure one's 'neighbour'. Caparo later added the three-stage duty test."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Torts","q":"The rule of absolute liability laid down in M.C. Mehta v. Union of India (1987) differs from Rylands v. Fletcher because it:","o":["requires proof of negligence by the enterprise","allows no exceptions such as act of God or act of a stranger","applies only to non-natural use of land by its owner","applies only where the hazardous substance escapes beyond the premises"],"a":1,"e":"In the Oleum gas leak case, the Court held that an enterprise engaged in a hazardous or inherently dangerous activity owes an absolute and non-delegable duty to the community and cannot plead the exceptions available under Rylands v. Fletcher."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property","q":"Under Section 54 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, a sale of tangible immovable property worth ₹100 or more can be made only by:","o":["delivery of possession alone","an oral agreement with part payment","a notarised affidavit","a registered instrument"],"a":3,"e":"Sale of such property needs a registered instrument. A mere agreement to sell does not create an interest in the property, and Suraj Lamp & Industries v. State of Haryana (2011) held that sales by General Power of Attorney or agreement do not transfer title."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Succession","q":"Which decision held that daughters are coparceners by birth in a Hindu Mitakshara coparcenary irrespective of whether the father was alive on 9 September 2005?","o":["Mary Roy v. State of Kerala (1986)","Prakash v. Phulavati (2016)","Githa Hariharan v. Reserve Bank of India (1999)","Vineeta Sharma v. Rakesh Sharma (2020)"],"a":3,"e":"A three-judge bench clarified the Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act, 2005 and overruled Prakash v. Phulavati to the extent it required the father to be alive."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Family Law","q":"Under the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Marriage) Act, 2019, pronouncing instant triple talaq is punishable with imprisonment up to:","o":["three years","five years","seven years","one year"],"a":0,"e":"The Act, enacted after Shayara Bano v. Union of India (2017), declares talaq-e-biddat void and illegal and makes the offence cognizable and non-bailable subject to Magistrate's bail, with imprisonment up to three years and fine."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property Rights","q":"Following the 44th Amendment, the right to property is now:","o":["a constitutional right under Article 300A","a fundamental right under Article 31","a fundamental right under Article 19(1)(f)","protected only by Article 31A"],"a":0,"e":"The 44th Amendment (1978) repealed Article 19(1)(f) and Article 31, and inserted Article 300A: no person shall be deprived of property except by authority of law."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Contract","q":"A threatens to commit suicide unless B signs an agreement to sell his land to A. The contract is:","o":["voidable only if A stood in a fiduciary relation with B","voidable at B's option on the ground of coercion","void ab initio, since it is without free consent","valid, since the threat is not directed against B or his property"],"a":1,"e":"Section 15 treats the threat to commit an act forbidden by the IPC/BNS, including suicide, as coercion (Chikham Amiraju v. Seshama). Under Section 19 the contract is voidable at the option of the coerced party."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Family Law","q":"In Mohd. Abdul Samad v. State of Telangana (2024), the Supreme Court held that:","o":["maintenance is limited to the iddat period in all circumstances","a divorced Muslim woman may claim maintenance under Section 125 CrPC (now BNSS Section 144)","she may claim maintenance only under the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986","the CrPC remedy applies only if she waives her personal law"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the secular provision applies to all married women irrespective of religion, and that the 1986 Act does not displace it, reaffirming the logic of Shah Bano and Danial Latifi."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Analytical School","q":"John Austin defined law as:","o":["a union of primary and secondary rules","an instrument of social engineering","a command of the sovereign backed by sanction","a prediction of what courts will do in fact"],"a":2,"e":"Austin's command theory of the Province of Jurisprudence Determined treats law as a general command of a determinate sovereign enforced by sanction."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Positivism","q":"The 'rule of recognition' as the test of validity of a legal system is associated with:","o":["H.L.A. Hart","Hans Kelsen","Lon Fuller","Ronald Dworkin"],"a":0,"e":"In The Concept of Law, Hart described law as a union of primary rules of obligation and secondary rules (recognition, change, adjudication), with the rule of recognition identifying valid law."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Pure Theory","q":"The concept of the 'Grundnorm' forms a part of the legal theory of:","o":["F.C. von Savigny","Roscoe Pound","Hans Kelsen","John Austin"],"a":2,"e":"Kelsen's Pure Theory of Law views the legal order as a hierarchy of norms whose validity traces back to a basic norm (Grundnorm), kept free from morality and sociology."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Historical School","q":"The view that law is a product of the Volksgeist (spirit of the people) and is found, not made, belongs to:","o":["F.C. von Savigny","Rudolf von Jhering","Oliver Wendell Holmes","Jeremy Bentham"],"a":0,"e":"Savigny, the leading figure of the Historical School, argued that law grows with the people's customs and consciousness rather than from legislative command."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Sociological School","q":"Law as 'social engineering', balancing competing interests, is associated with:","o":["Eugen Ehrlich","Karl Llewellyn","Roscoe Pound","Leon Duguit"],"a":2,"e":"Pound's sociological jurisprudence views law as a means of satisfying the maximum of wants with minimum friction, classifying individual, public and social interests."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Rights Analysis","q":"In Hohfeld's analysis of jural relations, the correlative of a 'right' (in the strict sense, a claim) is:","o":["duty","liability","liberty","immunity"],"a":0,"e":"Hohfeld's correlatives: right-duty, privilege (liberty)-no right, power-liability, immunity-disability."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Law and Morality","q":"The Hart-Fuller debate primarily concerned:","o":["the doctrine of separation of powers","the hierarchy of the sources of international law","the relationship between law and morality","the binding force of judicial precedent"],"a":2,"e":"The 1958 debate contrasted Hart's positivism (law and morals are conceptually separate) with Fuller's view that law has an inner morality, arising after the Nazi-era informer cases."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'res ipsa loquitur' means:","o":["the thing speaks for itself","the burden lies on him who asserts","the matter has already been decided","a thing lost belongs to the finder"],"a":0,"e":"In negligence, it allows the court to infer negligence from the very nature of the accident where the thing causing damage was under the defendant's control and the accident would not ordinarily happen without negligence."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'ubi jus ibi remedium' means:","o":["where there is a remedy, there is no right","where there is a right, there is a remedy","a remedy lapses with the death of the party","a right exists only if a statute provides it"],"a":1,"e":"It is the foundation of the idea that every legal wrong must have a remedy; Lord Holt's judgment in Ashby v. White is a classic illustration."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Natural Justice","q":"The rule against bias is expressed by the maxim:","o":["ignorantia juris non excusat","actus curiae neminem gravabit","nemo judex in causa sua","audi alteram partem"],"a":2,"e":"'No one should be a judge in his own cause' underpins the first limb of natural justice; 'audi alteram partem' (hear the other side) is the second. In Indian law the test is a real likelihood or reasonable apprehension of bias."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Precedent","q":"The part of a judgment that is binding as precedent is:","o":["the obiter dicta","the per incuriam portion","the sub silentio remarks","the ratio decidendi"],"a":3,"e":"The ratio is the principle of law necessary for the decision; obiter dicta are persuasive only, and decisions rendered per incuriam (ignoring a binding statute or precedent) lose binding force."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Justice Theory","q":"The concepts of the 'original position' and the 'veil of ignorance' belong to the theory of:","o":["Jeremy Bentham","John Rawls","Robert Nozick","Ronald Dworkin"],"a":1,"e":"In A Theory of Justice, Rawls argues that principles of justice are those chosen by rational persons behind a veil of ignorance about their own place in society."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Sources","q":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a primary source of international law in Article 38(1) of the ICJ Statute?","o":["Resolutions of the UN General Assembly","General principles of law recognised by civilised nations","International custom as evidence of a general practice accepted as law","International conventions establishing rules expressly recognised by States"],"a":0,"e":"Article 38(1)(a)-(c) list treaties, custom and general principles; judicial decisions and teachings of publicists are subsidiary means. UNGA resolutions are not listed, though they may evidence custom."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Law of the Sea","q":"Under UNCLOS (1982), the Exclusive Economic Zone of a coastal State extends up to how many nautical miles from the baseline?","o":["350","12","200","24"],"a":2,"e":"The territorial sea extends to 12 nm and the contiguous zone to 24 nm. The EEZ extends to 200 nm, while the continental shelf may extend up to 350 nm in certain cases."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"ICC","q":"Which of the following is NOT a State Party to the Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court?","o":["India","Brazil","Germany","Japan"],"a":0,"e":"India has not signed or ratified the Rome Statute. Germany, Japan and Brazil are States Parties."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Treaties","q":"A peremptory norm of general international law (jus cogens) is a norm from which:","o":["States may derogate by a bilateral treaty","derogation is allowed with Security Council approval","no derogation is permitted","derogation is allowed during armed conflict"],"a":2,"e":"Article 53 of the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties (1969) renders a treaty void if it conflicts with a peremptory norm accepted by the international community as a whole."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"ICJ","q":"In the Jadhav case (India v. Pakistan, 2019), the ICJ found that Pakistan had breached:","o":["Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations","Article III of the Genocide Convention of 1948","Article 4 of the Third Geneva Convention of 1949","Article 36 of the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that Pakistan failed to inform Kulbhushan Jadhav of his right to consular access and called for effective review and reconsideration of his conviction and sentence."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Treaties","q":"In April 2025, following the Pahalgam attack, India placed which treaty in abeyance?","o":["The Simla Agreement, 1972","The Kartarpur Corridor Agreement, 2019","The Tashkent Declaration, 1966","The Indus Waters Treaty, 1960"],"a":3,"e":"The 1960 treaty, brokered by the World Bank, allocates the eastern rivers to India and the western rivers to Pakistan. Its suspension has raised questions under the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, since the treaty has no unilateral exit clause."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Human Rights","q":"The 'International Bill of Human Rights' comprises:","o":["the Geneva Conventions and their Additional Protocols","the UN Charter, the ICCPR and the CAT","the UDHR, the ICCPR and the ICESCR","the UDHR, CEDAW and the CRC"],"a":2,"e":"The UDHR (1948) together with the two 1966 Covenants make up the International Bill of Human Rights."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Refugee Law","q":"Which statement about India and the 1951 Refugee Convention is correct?","o":["India has ratified the Convention but not the 1967 Protocol","India has ratified both the Convention and its 1967 Protocol","India has signed the Convention with reservations on non-refoulement","India is a party to neither the 1951 Convention nor the 1967 Protocol"],"a":3,"e":"India has no national refugee statute, and handles refugees through executive policy and the Foreigners Act, though non-refoulement is argued to flow from Article 21 and customary law."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Climate Law","q":"In its July 2025 advisory opinion on climate change, the ICJ concluded that:","o":["only developed States bear legally binding duties on climate protection","climate duties are only political commitments under the Paris Agreement","no rule of international law recognises a right to a healthy environment","States owe binding duties to protect the climate, and breach may entail reparation"],"a":3,"e":"The Court rejected the view that climate treaties are the only sources of obligation, finding that customary law and other treaties also bind States, and that internationally wrongful acts can give rise to legal consequences. (Verify details before the exam.)"},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Patents","q":"In Novartis AG v. Union of India (2013), the Supreme Court refused a patent for the beta crystalline form of imatinib mesylate by applying:","o":["Section 3(i) of the Patents Act, 1970","Section 2(1)(j) of the Patents Act, 1970","Section 3(d) of the Patents Act, 1970","Section 3(b) of the Patents Act, 1970"],"a":2,"e":"Section 3(d) denies patents to new forms of known substances unless they show enhanced therapeutic efficacy. The Court held the claimed compound did not."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Copyright","q":"Under the Copyright Act, 1957, the term of copyright in a published literary work is:","o":["the author's lifetime plus 50 years, counted from the next calendar year","60 years from the first publication of the work, irrespective of authorship","the author's lifetime plus 70 years, counted from the next calendar year","the author's lifetime plus 60 years, counted from the next calendar year"],"a":3,"e":"Section 22 prescribes life plus sixty years for original literary, dramatic, musical and artistic works, which is shorter than the life-plus-70 standard of many jurisdictions."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Data Protection","q":"The maximum monetary penalty under the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, for failure to take reasonable security safeguards to prevent a personal data breach, is:","o":["₹500 crore","₹250 crore","₹100 crore","₹50 crore"],"a":1,"e":"The Schedule provides penalties up to ₹250 crore for failure of reasonable security safeguards; up to ₹200 crore for failure to notify the Board and affected persons of a breach; and ₹200 crore for breach of children's data obligations."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Data Protection","q":"Under the DPDP Act, 2023, a 'Data Principal' is:","o":["the individual to whom the personal data relates","the Data Protection Board of India","a person who processes data only on behalf of another","the entity that determines the purpose and means of processing"],"a":0,"e":"The entity deciding purposes and means is the Data Fiduciary. A Data Processor acts on its behalf. For a child (under 18), the parent or lawful guardian acts as the Data Principal."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Competition","q":"The Competition (Amendment) Act, 2023 introduced a deal value threshold requiring CCI notification of transactions valued above:","o":["₹1,000 crore","₹5,000 crore","₹2,000 crore","₹500 crore"],"a":2,"e":"Combinations with deal value above ₹2,000 crore must be notified to the CCI if the target has substantial business operations in India, even if asset or turnover thresholds are not met. The amendment also cut the review period to 150 days."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Insolvency","q":"Under the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016, the Corporate Insolvency Resolution Process must be completed within a maximum of how many days, including extension and litigation time?","o":["330 days","365 days","180 days","270 days"],"a":0,"e":"The initial period is 180 days, extendable by 90 days, and the outer limit is 330 days including litigation (inserted by the 2019 amendment, and read as mandatory in Essar Steel, with the Supreme Court allowing extensions only in exceptional cases)."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 135 of the Companies Act, 2013, a covered company must spend on CSR at least what percentage of its average net profits of the three preceding financial years?","o":["5%","2%","1%","3%"],"a":1,"e":"CSR applies to companies with net worth of ₹500 crore or more, turnover of ₹1,000 crore or more, or net profit of ₹5 crore or more in the preceding financial year; unspent amounts are transferred to a specified fund."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Consumer Law","q":"As per the Consumer Protection (Jurisdiction of the District Commission, State Commission and National Commission) Rules, 2021, the pecuniary jurisdiction of a District Commission extends to consideration paid of up to:","o":["₹2 crore","₹1 crore","₹20 lakh","₹50 lakh"],"a":3,"e":"The 2019 Act originally fixed ₹1 crore for District Commissions, but the 2021 Rules revised it to ₹50 lakh (State Commission: ₹50 lakh to ₹2 crore; National Commission: above ₹2 crore), based on consideration paid."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Arbitration","q":"Section 16 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 empowering the tribunal to rule on its own jurisdiction reflects the principle of:","o":["kompetenz-kompetenz","lex loci arbitri","res judicata","forum non conveniens"],"a":0,"e":"Kompetenz-kompetenz lets the arbitral tribunal rule on objections to its own jurisdiction, including the existence or validity of the arbitration agreement."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Mediation","q":"Under the Mediation Act, 2023, a mediated settlement agreement is:","o":["final, binding and enforceable as if it were a court judgment or decree","enforceable only after notarisation by both parties","enforceable as an arbitral award under Section 36 of the Arbitration Act","enforceable only after confirmation by the High Court"],"a":0,"e":"Section 27 treats a registered mediated settlement agreement as enforceable in the manner of a judgment or decree of a court. The Act also provides for pre-litigation mediation and community mediation."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Online Gaming","q":"The Promotion and Regulation of Online Gaming Act, 2025 prohibits:","o":["all e-sports and all social games played online","online money games, whether of skill or chance, and their advertising","only those online games that are games of pure chance","every kind of online game for persons below 18 years"],"a":1,"e":"The Act bans online money games and facilitation of financial transactions for them while promoting e-sports and social gaming. (Verify the latest legal challenges before the exam.)"},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Administrative Law","q":"Under the Wednesbury principle of judicial review, a decision is open to challenge if it is:","o":["made in breach of the rule of audi alteram partem","made after failing to publish its reasons promptly","made without lawful jurisdiction over the subject matter","so unreasonable that no reasonable authority could ever have come to it"],"a":3,"e":"Associated Provincial Picture Houses v. Wednesbury Corporation (1948) set the standard of unreasonableness; Indian courts also apply proportionality in rights-based review."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"RTI","q":"Under the Right to Information Act, 2005, a Public Information Officer must ordinarily respond to a request within:","o":["60 days","15 days","45 days","30 days"],"a":3,"e":"The period is 30 days, reduced to 48 hours where the information concerns the life or liberty of a person, and extended to 35 days when the application goes through an Assistant PIO."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Environmental Law","q":"Which decision adopted the precautionary principle as part of Indian environmental law, reading it into Article 21 along with Articles 47, 48A and 51A(g)?","o":["Subhash Kumar v. State of Bihar (1991)","Vellore Citizens' Welfare Forum v. Union of India (1996)","Rural Litigation and Entitlement Kendra v. State of U.P. (1985)","M.C. Mehta v. Kamal Nath (1997)"],"a":1,"e":"Vellore Citizens' declared the precautionary principle and polluter pays principle as part of the law of the land. Kamal Nath is the public trust doctrine case; Subhash Kumar recognised pollution-free water and air as part of Article 21."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Environmental Law","q":"An appeal against an order of the National Green Tribunal lies directly to:","o":["the Chairperson of the NGT","the High Court of the concerned State","the Central Government","the Supreme Court of India"],"a":3,"e":"Section 22 of the National Green Tribunal Act, 2010 provides appeal to the Supreme Court within 90 days."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Environmental Law","q":"In M.K. Ranjitsinh v. Union of India (2024), the Supreme Court recognised a right against the adverse effects of climate change as flowing from:","o":["Articles 19 and 22","Articles 14 and 21","Articles 25 and 26","Articles 21A and 51A(g)"],"a":1,"e":"In the Great Indian Bustard case, the Court held that the right to a clean environment and the right to be free from climate change's adverse effects form part of the rights to life and equality."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Labour Law","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four Labour Codes consolidating central labour laws?","o":["The Code on Wages, 2019, dealing with wages and bonus","The Code on Employment and Skill Development, 2020","The Code on Social Security, 2020, dealing with social security","The Industrial Relations Code, 2020, dealing with trade unions"],"a":1,"e":"The four Codes are Wages (2019), Industrial Relations (2020), Social Security (2020), and Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions (2020); the Government brought them into force on 21 November 2025."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Gender Justice","q":"Guidelines against sexual harassment at the workplace, drawing on CEDAW and Articles 14, 15, 19 and 21, were first laid down in:","o":["Bodhisattwa Gautam v. Subhra Chakraborty (1996)","Gaurav Jain v. Union of India (1997)","Laxmi v. Union of India (2014)","Vishaka v. State of Rajasthan (1997)"],"a":3,"e":"The Vishaka guidelines operated as law until the Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (Prevention, Prohibition and Redressal) Act, 2013 (POSH Act)."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Legal Services","q":"An award of a Lok Adalat under the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987 is:","o":["advisory in nature, needing court confirmation","binding only on parties who consent after the award","deemed a decree of a civil court, final and binding with no appeal","appealable to the High Court within 30 days"],"a":2,"e":"Section 21 treats the award as a civil court decree, final and binding on all parties and not appealable; the remedy is a writ under Articles 226/32 in exceptional cases."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"POCSO","q":"In Just Rights for Children Alliance v. S. Harish (2024), the Supreme Court held that:","o":["the offence arises only when the material is held for commercial gain","private viewing of such material is not an offence under any statute","only persons who circulate such material to others are liable under POCSO","storing or viewing child sexual abuse material may be an offence even without transmission"],"a":3,"e":"The Court (Sept 2024) set aside a Madras High Court view and urged replacing the term 'child pornography' with 'child sexual exploitative and abuse material' (CSEAM)."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Judiciary","q":"Who is the Chief Justice of India as of November 2026?","o":["Justice B.R. Gavai","Justice Sanjiv Khanna","Justice Surya Kant","Justice D.Y. Chandrachud"],"a":2,"e":"Justice Surya Kant took oath as the 53rd CJI on 24 November 2025, succeeding Justice B.R. Gavai. His tenure runs until February 2027. (Verify before the exam.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Waqf","q":"The Waqf (Amendment) Act, 2025 renamed the Waqf Act, 1995 as:","o":["the Waqf Boards Modernisation and Accountability Act, 1995","the Waqf Properties Regulation and Protection Act, 1995","the Unified Waqf Management, Empowerment, Efficiency and Development Act, 1995","the Unified Waqf Trust Administration and Reform Act, 1995"],"a":2,"e":"The Act, abbreviated UMEED, is under constitutional challenge, and in 2025 the Supreme Court granted interim relief against certain provisions, such as the five-year practising-Muslim requirement."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Religious Places","q":"The Places of Worship (Special Provisions) Act, 1991 freezes the religious character of places of worship as it existed on:","o":["9 November 2019","6 December 1992","26 January 1950","15 August 1947"],"a":3,"e":"The Act bars conversion of a place of worship and keeps its 1947 character, excluding the Ram Janmabhoomi-Babri Masjid dispute. Its validity is pending before the Supreme Court."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Digital Rights","q":"In Amar Jain v. Union of India (2025), the Supreme Court recognised that digital access, in the context of KYC for acid attack survivors and persons with visual disabilities, is part of:","o":["the right to property under Article 300A","the freedom of trade under Article 19(1)(g) alone","the right to life and liberty under Article 21","the cultural rights under Article 30"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that digital accessibility is integral to dignified life and directed accessible KYC norms."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Telecom Law","q":"The Telecommunications Act, 2023 repealed which colonial-era statute?","o":["The Telecom Regulatory Authority of India Act, 1997","The Information Technology Act, 2000","The Cable Television Networks (Regulation) Act, 1995","The Indian Telegraph Act, 1885"],"a":3,"e":"The 2023 Act replaced the Indian Telegraph Act, 1885 and the Indian Wireless Telegraphy Act, 1933. The Post Office Act, 2023 separately replaced the 1898 Act."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Judiciary","q":"The retirement ages of Supreme Court judges and High Court judges are, respectively:","o":["62 years and 60 years","65 years and 62 years","67 years and 62 years","65 years and 65 years"],"a":1,"e":"Article 124(2) fixes 65 years for Supreme Court judges and Article 217(1) fixes 62 years for High Court judges."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Personal Liberty","q":"Under Article 22(4), preventive detention beyond what period requires the opinion of an Advisory Board?","o":["Three months","Six months","Twelve months","One month"],"a":0,"e":"Article 22(4) permits detention beyond three months only with an Advisory Board report. The 44th Amendment sought to reduce this to two months but has not been brought into force."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Constitutional Amendments","q":"The Constitution (130th Amendment) Bill, 2025, introduced in Parliament in August 2025, proposes to:","o":["replace the Collegium with a judicial appointments commission","extend the term of the Lok Sabha to six years","lower the minimum age to contest Lok Sabha elections to 21 years","remove a PM, CM or minister held in custody for 30 consecutive days on serious charges"],"a":3,"e":"The Bill, referred to a Joint Parliamentary Committee, provides for removal of a Minister, Chief Minister or Prime Minister arrested and detained for 30 consecutive days for offences punishable with five or more years. (Verify its current status.)"}],"b":[{"id":"S1","m":25,"w":450,"q":"Governors and the President hold constitutional power over assent to State Bills, yet delays have triggered litigation. Critically examine whether courts can impose timelines on constitutional authorities, with reference to the Presidential Reference of 2025 and the separation of powers.","o":["Constitutional scheme: Articles 200, 201, 163 and 361; the three options of a Governor.","Rival positions: judicially fixed timelines and deemed assent versus the text and the political-question limits on courts.","Treat the 2025 opinion as the central authority: no deemed assent and no rigid timelines, but limited review for prolonged and unexplained inaction.","Federalism and democratic accountability (Punchhi Commission, Sarkaria Commission, Shamsher Singh).","A reasoned conclusion, such as guidelines on reasons and time frames short of judicial legislation."]},{"id":"S2","m":25,"w":450,"q":"The BNS, BNSS and BSA replaced the colonial criminal codes in July 2024. Evaluate whether the new codes strike a balance between efficient criminal justice and protection of individual liberty, citing at least three provisions.","o":["Aims: decolonisation, victim-centric reforms, technology (e-FIR, electronic evidence, forensic mandate, audio-video recording).","Pro-efficiency: time limits (judgment in 45 days), Zero FIR, community service, trial in absentia.","Liberty concerns: 15-day police custody spread over 40/60 days, Section 152 BNS (post-sedition), organised crime and terrorism definitions, handcuffing.","Judicial safeguards: Arnesh Kumar, D.K. Basu, Article 21 and Maneka Gandhi fairness review.","Conclusion: structurally promising, with outcomes depending on implementation and judicial scrutiny."]}]};
